VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Noncumulative preferred with no dividend declaration
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when noncumulative preferred stock is outstanding and no dividends are declared during the period, what amount is deducted from net income in the basic EPS numerator?', '["The full annual noncumulative preferred dividend entitlement", "Zero, because noncumulative dividends need not be deducted unless declared", "An amount equal to the par value of the preferred stock", "The accumulated preferred dividend arrearage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only declared dividends on noncumulative preferred stock are deducted from net income for basic EPS. Unlike cumulative preferred stock, where the full periodic dividend is deducted regardless of declaration, noncumulative preferred creates no obligation until declared. Choice A is incorrect because it applies the cumulative preferred rule to noncumulative stock.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Induced conversion expense effect on EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Grantham Corp. offers convertible bondholders an additional 2,000 shares beyond the original conversion terms to induce conversion. On the conversion date, the market price of Grantham''s stock is $40 per share. Under ASC 470-20-40, what is the effect of this inducement on basic EPS?', '["No effect on the EPS numerator because conversions do not affect earnings", "A $80,000 charge is deducted from the basic EPS numerator as a conversion inducement expense", "The inducement increases the denominator but does not affect the numerator", "The $80,000 is added back to the numerator under the if-converted method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 470-20-40-16, the fair value of the additional securities issued beyond the original conversion terms is recognized as an inducement expense. Per ASC 260-10-45-11, this inducement charge ($2,000 shares x $40 = $80,000) reduces the basic EPS numerator. Choice C is incorrect because induced conversions directly reduce the numerator, not just expand the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Partially paid subscription shares proportion-based inclusion
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Holbrook Inc. issues 30,000 shares under a stock subscription on February 1. Subscribers pay 50% of the $60 per share price at issuance, with the remaining 50% collected on August 1. Under ASC 260-10-45-13, what equivalent shares are included in the weighted-average denominator from February 1 through July 31?', '["30,000 shares, because all subscribed shares are counted from issuance", "Zero shares, because partially paid shares are excluded entirely until fully paid", "15,000 share-equivalents, based on the 50% proportion paid", "30,000 shares weighted at half their value, producing 15,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, partially paid shares are included in the weighted-average denominator to the extent they participate in dividends, which corresponds to the proportion paid. At 50% paid, 30,000 x 50% = 15,000 equivalent shares from February 1 through July 31; then 30,000 from August 1 onward. Choice A is incorrect because full inclusion requires full payment. Choice D describes the same result but uses flawed reasoning about weighting value.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Antidilutive test: ordering multiple convertible securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. has three convertible securities: Bond A adds $90,000 after-tax interest and 30,000 shares (incremental EPS $3.00), Bond B adds $200,000 and 50,000 shares ($4.00), and Preferred C adds $120,000 dividends and 45,000 shares ($2.67). Basic EPS is $3.50. Evaluate the order in which these securities should be tested for dilution under ASC 260-10-45-21.', '["All three should be included simultaneously to determine diluted EPS", "Bond A first, then Preferred C, then Bond B — ranked from most to least dilutive", "Preferred C first, then Bond A, then Bond B — ranked from lowest to highest incremental EPS", "Bond B first because it adds the most shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities must be tested individually in order from most dilutive to least dilutive (lowest incremental EPS to highest). Preferred C ($2.67) is tested first, then Bond A ($3.00), then Bond B ($4.00). Each security is included only if it further dilutes the cumulative EPS. Bond B at $4.00 exceeds basic EPS of $3.50 and would be antidilutive. Choice A is incorrect because simultaneous inclusion could mask an antidilutive security that should be excluded.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — EPS effect of share-based payment modification that increases fair value
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Larkfield Corp. modifies 15,000 unvested stock options, increasing the exercise price from $30 to $25 and adding one year to the vesting period. The modification increases total fair value by $90,000. The average market price is $50 and there is $120,000 of total unrecognized compensation cost after modification. Analyze how the modification changes the diluted EPS treasury stock method computation.', '["No change; option modifications do not affect diluted EPS until the options vest", "The assumed proceeds decrease because the lower exercise price reduces cash the entity would receive upon exercise", "The assumed proceeds increase because the additional unrecognized compensation cost raises assumed proceeds, partially offsetting the lower exercise price", "The modification converts the options from equity-classified to liability-classified, removing them from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method include the exercise price plus average unrecognized compensation cost. After modification, the exercise price drops to $25 but unrecognized cost increases to $120,000 / 15,000 = $8 per option. Total assumed proceeds per share = $25 + $8 = $33. Before modification, if unrecognized cost were lower with the $30 exercise price, the net effect depends on both components. The key insight is that higher unrecognized compensation partially offsets the lower exercise price. Choice A is incorrect because unvested options are included in diluted EPS if dilutive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Liability-classified SARs: only share-settled affect denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgecrest Corp. grants 20,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) under a plan requiring mandatory cash settlement. The SARs are liability-classified under ASC 718. The controller proposes adding incremental shares to diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Identify the flaw in the controller''s proposal.', '["No flaw; all SARs are included in diluted EPS regardless of settlement form", "The flaw is that SARs should use the if-converted method rather than the treasury stock method", "The flaw is that cash-settled SARs do not result in share issuance and therefore produce zero incremental shares for diluted EPS", "The flaw is that only exercised SARs should be included in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-31, only equity-classified instruments that will be settled in shares affect the diluted EPS denominator. Cash-settled SARs are liability-classified; their compensation cost flows through the income statement (reducing the numerator) but no shares are issued, so zero incremental shares enter the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because settlement form determines whether the instrument creates potential common shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — OCI items are not included in EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following is excluded from the numerator when computing basic earnings per share?', '["Income from continuing operations", "Gain on discontinued operations", "Unrealized gains on available-for-sale debt securities reported in other comprehensive income", "Loss from continuing operations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-5, the EPS numerator uses net income (or income from continuing operations). Items reported in other comprehensive income (OCI), such as unrealized gains on available-for-sale debt securities, bypass net income and do not enter the EPS computation. Choice A is incorrect because income from continuing operations is the primary EPS numerator component for entities with discontinued operations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Reverse stock split requires retroactive denominator restatement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On October 1, 2025, Stratton Holdings effects a 1-for-4 reverse stock split, reducing outstanding shares from 800,000 to 200,000. The company reports annual net income of $600,000 and has no preferred stock. What weighted-average shares should Stratton use for basic EPS in 2025?', '["350,000 shares, weighting pre-split and post-split shares", "200,000 shares, applying the reverse split retroactively to the full year", "500,000 shares, weighting 800,000 for nine months and 200,000 for three months", "800,000 shares, because the reverse split is applied prospectively"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12A, reverse stock splits are applied retroactively to all periods presented as if the split occurred at the beginning of the earliest period. The 200,000 post-split shares are used for the entire year, producing basic EPS of $600,000 / 200,000 = $3.00. Choice C is incorrect because weighting would only apply to share changes involving new consideration (like issuances), not to stock splits or reverse splits.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Discontinued operations require separate EPS line
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, when a public entity reports a discontinued operation, how must EPS be presented for the discontinued component?', '["No separate EPS is required for discontinued operations", "EPS for discontinued operations may be disclosed only in the notes", "A separate per-share amount for discontinued operations must appear on the face of the income statement or in the notes", "Discontinued operations EPS is combined with continuing operations into a single line"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, when an entity reports discontinued operations, per-share amounts for the discontinued component must be presented either on the face of the income statement or disclosed in the notes. The entity cannot simply omit the discontinued operations EPS. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires separate EPS disclosure for material discontinued operations to help users understand earnings composition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Interim EPS uses year-to-date weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260 and ASC 270, when a public entity computes year-to-date basic EPS for an interim report covering the first two quarters, what share count is used as the denominator?', '["The shares outstanding at the end of the second quarter", "The weighted-average shares for the year-to-date period from the beginning of the fiscal year", "The simple average of Q1 shares and Q2 shares", "The shares outstanding at the beginning of the fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and ASC 270, year-to-date interim EPS uses the cumulative weighted-average shares for the entire period from the first day of the fiscal year through the end of the interim period. This is not a simple average of quarterly share counts. Choice C is incorrect because averaging quarterly weighted-average figures would produce incorrect results when share counts change mid-period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Contingently returnable shares issued in acquisition remain in denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellrose Corp. issues 80,000 common shares to acquire Juniper Inc. on March 1, 2025. The agreement provides that if Juniper''s EBITDA falls below $2,000,000 in Year 1, the sellers must return 20,000 shares. As of December 31, 2025, Juniper''s EBITDA is $2,400,000. Analyze the treatment of the 80,000 shares in Bellrose''s 2025 basic EPS denominator.', '["Include only 60,000 shares from March 1 because 20,000 are contingently returnable", "Include all 80,000 shares from March 1 because they are legally outstanding and the return contingency has not been triggered", "Include 80,000 shares from January 1 because acquisition shares are treated retroactively", "Include 60,000 shares from March 1 and add the contingent 20,000 only in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48 and ASC 260-10-45-13, shares issued in an acquisition that are subject to return upon failure to meet a contingency are included in basic EPS from the issuance date because they are legally outstanding. Unlike contingently issuable shares (not yet issued), contingently returnable shares have already been issued and are counted. Since the EBITDA threshold was met, no return occurs, but even if it were pending, the shares would remain in the denominator until actually returned. Choice A is incorrect because the contingent return feature does not reduce the share count while the shares remain outstanding.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Minimum EPS precision (two decimal places)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under SEC Regulation S-X and common practice for public entities, to what minimum precision must earnings per share amounts be presented?', '["To the nearest whole dollar", "To the nearest cent (two decimal places)", "To the nearest tenth of a cent (three decimal places)", "To four decimal places"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SEC Regulation S-X Rule 11-02 and common practice, EPS amounts for public entities are presented to at least two decimal places (the nearest cent). Rounding to the nearest whole dollar (Choice A) would sacrifice meaningful precision, while three or four decimal places exceed what is customarily required. Most registrants follow the two-decimal convention consistent with SEC staff expectations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Purchased call option on own shares is antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Riverstone Corp. purchases call options on 20,000 shares of its own common stock at a $45 strike price. The average market price is $60. The CFO proposes including these purchased calls in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method, arguing they are in the money. Analyze whether the CFO''s proposal is correct.', '["Correct; in-the-money purchased calls are always dilutive", "Incorrect; purchased calls on the entity''s own shares are always excluded from diluted EPS because they are antidilutive by nature", "Correct; purchased calls are treated identically to written calls", "Incorrect; purchased calls affect only basic EPS, not diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, a purchased call option on the entity''s own shares is always antidilutive. Exercising a purchased call requires the entity to pay cash to receive its own shares, which would increase EPS (fewer shares outstanding for the same income). Therefore, purchased calls are excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because the in-the-money status of a purchased call does not make it dilutive from the entity''s EPS perspective.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — EPS denominator uses common shares, not total equity shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornbury Corp. has 250,000 common shares and 100,000 nonconvertible preferred shares outstanding for the entire year. Net income is $900,000 and preferred dividends are $200,000. An analyst divides $700,000 by 350,000 total shares to compute EPS of $2.00. Is this correct?', '["Yes; all classes of equity shares are included in the EPS denominator", "No; only common shares are used in the denominator, producing basic EPS of $2.80", "Yes; because the preferred stock is nonconvertible, it is combined with common for EPS", "No; the preferred shares should be included but at a reduced weighting based on dividend priority"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the denominator for basic EPS includes only the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding. Nonconvertible preferred shares are never included in the denominator. The correct computation is ($900,000 - $200,000) / 250,000 = $2.80. Choice A is incorrect because EPS measures earnings per common share, not earnings per total equity share.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Increasing-rate preferred stock imputed discount
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Marcellus Corp. issues 10,000 shares of increasing-rate preferred stock that pays $1 per share in Year 1, $3 in Year 2, and $5 in Year 3 and beyond. The issuance price is $50 per share. Under EITF 85-17 (codified in ASC 260), the below-market dividends in Years 1 and 2 create an imputed discount. The controller deducts only the $10,000 declared Year 1 dividend from the EPS numerator. Evaluate this approach.', '["Correct; only declared dividends are deducted for preferred stock", "Incorrect; the imputed discount should be amortized as an additional charge to the EPS numerator over the period to the first call date", "Correct; increasing-rate preferred is treated identically to fixed-rate preferred", "Incorrect; the full Year 3 steady-state dividend of $50,000 should be deducted in every year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-2 (EITF 85-17), increasing-rate preferred stock with below-market initial dividends requires an imputed discount. This discount is amortized as a charge against the EPS numerator over the period from issuance to the earliest redemption date, in addition to the declared dividends. Choice A is incorrect because the imputed discount creates an incremental EPS numerator reduction beyond the cash dividends actually declared.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Accelerated share repurchase initial delivery
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, 2025, Westfield Corp. enters an accelerated share repurchase (ASR) agreement for $3,000,000 and receives an initial delivery of 50,000 shares. Final settlement occurs in October when 12,000 additional shares are delivered. For basic EPS, from what date are the initially delivered 50,000 shares excluded from the weighted-average?', '["January 1, 2025, applied retroactively like a reverse stock split", "July 1, 2025, treated as a treasury stock purchase on the trade date", "October 1, 2025, recognized only at final settlement", "The shares are not excluded; they remain in the denominator until retired"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99 (SEC Staff guidance), the initial share delivery in an ASR is treated as a treasury stock retirement on the trade date. The 50,000 shares are removed from the weighted-average denominator beginning July 1. The forward component (additional 12,000 shares) is separately accounted for upon final settlement. Choice A is incorrect because ASR transactions are not retroactive like stock splits; they involve cash consideration.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — DRIP shares weighted from issuance, not retroactively
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. has 500,000 shares outstanding on January 1. Through its dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP), 4,000 new shares are issued each quarter on March 31, June 30, September 30, and December 31. An accountant applies the DRIP shares retroactively to January 1, treating them like stock dividends. Is this treatment correct?', '["Yes; DRIP shares are analogous to stock dividends and should be retroactively applied", "No; DRIP shares are issued for consideration and should be weighted from each quarterly issuance date", "Yes; because DRIP shares are distributed to existing shareholders at no additional cost", "No; DRIP shares should be excluded from the weighted-average entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively because no new consideration enters the entity. DRIP shares, however, are issued in exchange for reinvested cash dividends, which constitutes consideration. They are therefore treated like any other share issuance and weighted from each respective issuance date. Choice A is incorrect because the presence of consideration (reinvested dividends) distinguishes DRIP issuances from noncash stock dividends.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Down-round provision on warrants triggers deemed dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornwell Corp. has 50,000 outstanding equity-classified warrants with an exercise price of $30. A down-round provision is triggered when Thornwell issues new stock at $22, reducing the warrant exercise price to $22. The fair value change attributable to the down-round trigger is $160,000. Under ASU 2017-11, analyze the effect on EPS.', '["No EPS effect; the down-round merely changes the diluted EPS denominator computation", "The warrants must be reclassified from equity to liability, with fair value changes in earnings", "The $160,000 is recognized as a deemed dividend, reducing the basic EPS numerator", "The $160,000 increases additional paid-in capital with no income statement or EPS impact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASU 2017-11 (ASC 260-10-S99-2), when a down-round provision is triggered, the value transferred to warrant holders (measured as the change in fair value immediately before and after the trigger) is recognized as a deemed dividend. This $160,000 deemed dividend reduces income available to common shareholders for basic EPS. Choice B is incorrect because ASU 2017-11 specifically allows equity classification to be maintained despite the down-round feature.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Subsidiary potential common shares affect consolidated diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westfield Holdings owns 80% of Creekmore Inc. Creekmore has 200,000 shares outstanding and 10,000 dilutive employee stock options. If Creekmore''s options were exercised, Westfield''s effective ownership would decline. The consolidated controller omits this subsidiary-level dilution from consolidated diluted EPS. What error does the controller make?', '["No error; subsidiary dilutive securities never affect the parent''s consolidated EPS", "The error is that the controller should convert Creekmore''s options into equivalent parent shares", "The controller should include Creekmore''s options only in Creekmore''s standalone EPS", "The error is that ASC 260-10-45-26 requires subsidiary potential common shares to be reflected in consolidated diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, the dilutive effect of a subsidiary''s potential common shares must be reflected in the parent''s consolidated diluted EPS. When the subsidiary''s options dilute its share count, the parent''s proportionate claim on subsidiary earnings decreases. Ignoring this effect overstates consolidated diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly addresses how subsidiary-level dilution flows through to the consolidated computation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Stock splits applied retroactively in EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12A, when a stock split occurs during the year, how is the weighted-average number of shares adjusted for basic EPS?', '["The split is applied only to shares outstanding after the split date", "The split is applied retroactively as if it occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented", "The split is applied prospectively from the effective date", "The split has no effect on the weighted-average because no new consideration is received"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12A, stock splits (and stock dividends) are applied retroactively to all periods presented because no new resources flow into the entity. The weighted-average is restated as though the split occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented, ensuring comparability. Choice C is incorrect because prospective application would distort EPS comparisons between periods.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Restricted stock with forfeitable dividends excluded from basic EPS until vested
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverton Corp. grants 40,000 restricted shares to employees on January 1, 2025, with a three-year cliff vesting schedule. Dividends on the restricted shares are forfeitable if the employee terminates before vesting. Under ASC 260-10-45-13, analyze how these restricted shares affect basic EPS in 2025.', '["Include all 40,000 shares in the basic EPS denominator because they are legally outstanding", "Exclude from basic EPS; include as dilutive securities in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method", "Include only the vested portion (one-third) in the basic EPS denominator", "Exclude from both basic and diluted EPS until the three-year vesting period ends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, unvested restricted shares with forfeitable dividend rights are not participating securities and are excluded from basic EPS. They are treated as potential common shares for diluted EPS purposes, with unrecognized compensation cost included in assumed proceeds under the treasury stock method (ASC 260-10-45-29). Choice A is incorrect because, despite being legally outstanding, restricted shares with forfeitable dividends lack the participation rights needed for basic EPS inclusion before vesting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Preferred stock issuance costs reduce EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartfield Corp. issues preferred stock for $5,000,000 and incurs $200,000 in issuance costs charged directly against the preferred stock proceeds. The preferred stock pays an annual dividend of $300,000. Net income is $2,000,000 with 500,000 common shares. The controller deducts only the $300,000 dividend from the EPS numerator. Is any additional deduction required?', '["No; only declared preferred dividends affect the EPS numerator", "Yes; the $200,000 issuance costs should also be deducted from the EPS numerator as a return to preferred shareholders", "No; issuance costs are a direct charge to equity with no EPS impact", "Yes; but only if the preferred stock is mandatorily redeemable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-2, preferred stock issuance costs that are charged against the carrying amount of the preferred stock do not separately affect the EPS numerator at issuance. Only declared dividends, accretions, and redemption premiums reduce the numerator. The issuance costs reduce APIC or the preferred stock account but are not treated as a deemed dividend. Choice B is incorrect because issuance costs, unlike redemption premiums, do not represent a transfer of value to preferred shareholders.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Midyear equity issuance to settle litigation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On August 1, 2025, Kenworth Corp. issues 25,000 new common shares to settle a lawsuit, in lieu of paying $1,250,000 in damages. Kenworth had 500,000 shares outstanding from January 1. No other share changes occur during the year. What is the effect on the 2025 weighted-average denominator for basic EPS?', '["Zero effect; shares issued for noncash consideration are excluded from the denominator", "An increase of 25,000 shares for the full year because litigation settlements are retroactive", "An increase of approximately 10,417 shares, weighting the 25,000 shares for 5 months out of 12", "An increase of 25,000 shares from August 1 through December 31, contributing 10,417 to the weighted average"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued during the year are included in the weighted-average from the date of issuance, regardless of whether the consideration is cash, property, or services. The 25,000 shares issued August 1 are weighted for 5/12 of the year: 25,000 x 5/12 = 10,417 added to the weighted-average. Choice A is incorrect because shares issued for noncash consideration are included from the issuance date just like cash-issued shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Bonus issue (capitalization of retained earnings) applied retroactively
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On November 15, 2025, Calvert Corp.''s board capitalizes $1,200,000 of retained earnings by issuing one additional share for every four shares held ("bonus issue"). Before the bonus issue, 400,000 shares were outstanding. Under ASC 260-10-45-12A, what weighted-average shares should Calvert use for 2025 basic EPS?', '["400,000 shares for January through November, then 500,000 shares for the remainder", "500,000 shares for the full year, because bonus issues are applied retroactively", "450,000 shares, weighting the pre-bonus and post-bonus periods", "400,000 shares, because the bonus issue is a return of equity to shareholders"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12A, a bonus issue (capitalization of retained earnings without new consideration) is treated like a stock dividend. The additional 100,000 shares (400,000 / 4) are applied retroactively to the entire year. The weighted-average becomes 500,000 for all periods presented. Choice A is incorrect because, unlike share issuances for cash, bonus issues involve no new consideration and therefore receive retroactive treatment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Preferred stock converted below carrying amount: discount accreted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverford Industries issues mandatorily redeemable preferred stock at $50 per share with a $65 redemption price, creating a $15 per-share discount to be accreted over five years. In Year 2, the company converts 8,000 shares to common stock when the accreted carrying amount is $56 per share. The controller records the conversion at carrying amount and makes no EPS adjustment. Evaluate this approach for the year of conversion.', '["Correct; conversion at carrying amount has no EPS impact", "Incorrect; the unaccreted discount of $72,000 ($9 x 8,000 shares) should be recognized immediately as a deemed dividend reducing the EPS numerator", "Correct; accretion charges affect only future periods", "Incorrect; the $72,000 should be added back to the numerator because it reduces the preferred obligation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-2, when mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is converted before its scheduled redemption date, the remaining unaccreted discount ($65 - $56 = $9 x 8,000 = $72,000) is treated as a deemed dividend in the period of conversion. This accelerated accretion reduces income available to common shareholders for basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because early conversion triggers immediate recognition of the remaining discount, not deferral.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Mid-year change in tax rate affects if-converted add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. has $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds outstanding all year. A new tax law enacted on July 1 reduces the corporate rate from 28% to 21%. Pre-tax interest expense is $60,000. For diluted EPS using the if-converted method, what is the correct after-tax interest add-back to the numerator?', '["$43,200, calculated using the original 28% rate for the full year", "$47,400, calculated using the 21% rate for the full year", "$45,300, blending the 28% rate for six months and 21% rate for six months", "$60,000, because tax effects are ignored in EPS adjustments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, the if-converted method adds back interest expense net of income taxes. When the tax rate changes mid-year, the after-tax interest must reflect the blended effective rate actually applied during the period. First half: $30,000 x (1 - 0.28) = $21,600; second half: $30,000 x (1 - 0.21) = $23,700; total = $45,300. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the enacted rate change. Choice B similarly misstates by applying only the new rate.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L3 — Excess tax benefits excluded from treasury stock method after ASU 2016-09
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prior to ASU 2016-09, Glenridge Corp. included $45,000 of estimated excess tax benefits from stock option exercises in the assumed proceeds when applying the treasury stock method for diluted EPS. After adopting ASU 2016-09, the controller continues this practice. Analyze whether the controller''s approach remains correct.', '["Correct; excess tax benefits are still included in assumed proceeds under the treasury stock method", "Incorrect; ASU 2016-09 eliminated the inclusion of excess tax benefits from assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method", "Correct; but only if the entity has a history of generating excess tax benefits", "Incorrect; excess tax benefits now increase the EPS numerator instead of affecting the denominator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASU 2016-09 (codified in ASC 718-10-25), excess tax benefits from stock compensation are no longer recognized in APIC but flow through the income statement. Correspondingly, ASC 260-10-45-29 was updated to exclude excess tax benefits from assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds now include only the exercise price plus unamortized compensation cost. Choice A is incorrect because it reflects the pre-ASU 2016-09 guidance that has been superseded.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Options out of the money are antidilutive and excluded
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. has 25,000 employee stock options with a $70 exercise price and $8 of unrecognized compensation cost per option. The average market price during 2025 is $55. The controller includes these options in diluted EPS, arguing the unrecognized compensation adds to assumed proceeds. Analyze whether these options are dilutive.', '["Dilutive; the unrecognized compensation increases assumed proceeds sufficiently to produce incremental shares", "Antidilutive; because the exercise price alone exceeds the average market price, no incremental shares result under the treasury stock method", "Dilutive; all outstanding employee options must be included in diluted EPS regardless of exercise price", "Antidilutive; but only because the options have not yet vested"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, the treasury stock method uses assumed proceeds equal to the exercise price plus unrecognized compensation cost. Total assumed proceeds = ($70 + $8) x 25,000 = $1,950,000. Hypothetical shares repurchased = $1,950,000 / $55 = 35,455. Since 35,455 repurchased shares exceed the 25,000 options exercised, the net effect is a reduction in shares (antidilutive). The options are excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because even adding compensation cost, assumed proceeds per share ($78) still exceed the $55 market price, making the options antidilutive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Year-to-date vs. quarter EPS: interim cannot simply average quarterly figures
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarcliff Corp. reports Q1 basic EPS of $0.80 and Q2 basic EPS of $1.20. The CFO computes first-half basic EPS as the average ($0.80 + $1.20) / 2 = $1.00. Q1 weighted-average shares were 200,000 and Q2 weighted-average shares were 250,000 due to a May share issuance. Evaluate the CFO''s methodology.', '["Correct; averaging quarterly EPS is the accepted method for year-to-date figures", "Incorrect; year-to-date EPS must be computed from cumulative year-to-date income divided by the year-to-date weighted-average shares", "Correct; but only if the share counts are the same in both quarters", "Incorrect; the higher quarterly EPS should always be used as the year-to-date figure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260 and ASC 270, year-to-date EPS is computed independently using cumulative income and the cumulative weighted-average share count, not by averaging per-quarter EPS figures. When share counts change between quarters, averaging quarterly EPS produces a mathematically incorrect result. Year-to-date shares = (200,000 x 3 + 250,000 x 3) / 6 = 225,000. Year-to-date income = $160,000 + $300,000 = $460,000. Correct EPS = $460,000 / 225,000 = $2.04, not $1.00. Choice A is incorrect because quarterly EPS amounts have different denominators and cannot be simply averaged.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Simple capital structure presents only basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what EPS presentation is required for a public entity that has only common stock outstanding with no options, warrants, or convertible securities?', '["Both basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement", "Only basic EPS on the face of the income statement", "Only diluted EPS on the face of the income statement", "EPS presentation is optional for entities with simple capital structures"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, an entity with a simple capital structure (no potentially dilutive securities) presents only basic EPS on the face of the income statement. Since diluted EPS would equal basic EPS, a separate diluted figure is not required. Choice A is incorrect because dual presentation is required only for entities with complex capital structures (those with potentially dilutive securities outstanding).', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
